\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Quaternionic paths retain their order}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Take the one-vertex cellular two-torus. Let the binary tetrahedral
group #{2T} of [Hurwitz units](fgap-000K) act by left multiplication on
#{F=\mathbb Q\langle1,\qi,\qj,\qk\rangle}, with
#{\qi^2=\qj^2=\qk^2=-1} and #{\qi\qj=\qk=-\qj\qi}. Set the two positive loop
links to #{\qi} and #{\qj}. The inverse pullbacks are left
multiplication by #{-\qi} and #{-\qj}. Consequently
##{T_1^UT_2^U=L_{\qk},\qquad T_2^UT_1^U=L_{-\qk},\qquad
[T_1^U,T_2^U]=L_{2\qk}\ne0.}
Here #{L_h} denotes left multiplication by #{h}. The two paths
have the same initial and final vertex, yet their transports
disagree. By \ref{fdip-00SL}, #{\extd_U^2} is nonzero.}

\p{The Hurwitz lattice is preserved by these links. The present
calculation is over its rational span and uses no assertion
about an integral unimodular norm pairing.}
